Step 1: Initialize variables.
    • Create StringBuilder sb to build repeated string
    • Initialize count = 0 → tracks how many times string a is appended
Step 2: Build repeated string until it reaches target size.
    • While sb.length() < b.length():
      ○ Append string a to sb → sb.append(a)
      ○ Increment count → count++
    • Reason: b can only be a substring if it fits inside repeated a
Step 3: Check if b exists in current string.
    • If sb.indexOf(b) != -1:
      ○ Return count
Step 4: Append one more copy of a.
    • sb.append(a) → ensures overlap case is covered
Step 5: Check again for substring.
    • If sb.indexOf(b) != -1:
      ○ Return count + 1
Step 6: If not found.
    • Return -1 (b cannot be formed)
Time Complexity Analysis:
    • While loop appends string a until length ≥ m → takes O(m / n) appends, each append costs O(n), so total O(m)
    • First indexOf(b) runs on string of size ≈ m → O(m × m) = O(m²)
    • One extra append of a → O(n)
    • Second indexOf(b) runs on string of size ≈ (m + n) → O((m + n) × m)
    • Total Time Complexity → O(m + m² + n + (m + n) × m) = O((m + n) × m)
Space Complexity Analysis:
    • StringBuilder stores repeated string up to size ≈ (m + n) → O(m + n)
    • No other auxiliary data structures used
    • Total Space Complexity → O(m + n)















==========> Code <==========


class Solution {
    public int repeatedStringMatch(String a, String b) {
        StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();
        int cout=0;

        while(sb.length()<b.length()){
            sb.append(a);
            cout++;
        }

        if(sb.indexOf(b)!=-1){
            return cout;
        }

        sb.append(a);

        if(sb.indexOf(b)!=-1){
            return cout+1;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
